+### 2026-07-30 — Fixed go-v1 compile-driver cycle-4 review finds an unbound interpreter runtime
+- REVIEWER: `TASK-260720-2g21eg` cycle 4 on CocoaSkills base `495ad021847529ce5a544dba415ca2fe19949539`; the clean local main clone and live `origin/main` matched, both dependency handoffs were accepted, and the reviewed wheel matched the candidate `go_v1.py` and `cli.py` hashes. No CocoaSkills product or test file was modified.
+- RUNTIME-IMAGE FINDING: the installed macOS worker binds the 52 KB Python launcher but not the owner-writable 5.4 MB `Python.framework/.../Python` Mach-O image that `otool -L` proves actually executes it. That image is absent from the identity request/proof/post-exec set and direct retained watches; a native kqueue reproduction confirmed that watching only its containing directory does not detect an in-place child-content write. The claimed pre-launch, manager-owned-worker, and post-exec identity controls therefore remain open.
+- WINDOWS-VENV FINDING: the Windows path treats `<venv>/Scripts/python.exe`, `<venv>/Lib`, and the venv root as a complete private interpreter. A standard CPython Windows venv instead uses `venvlauncher.exe` plus the base installation selected by `pyvenv.cfg`, where the actual Python DLL and standard library remain. The current resolver either rejects the ordinary wheel-in-venv layout before launch or binds the wrong runtime while omitting executing base-runtime code.
+- GREEN GATES: focused pytest passed 295 / skipped 4; full pytest passed 968 / skipped 6; the standalone wheel-installed native macOS fixture passed 8; strict mypy passed 62 source files; diff, tabnanny, whitespace, and conflict-marker hygiene passed.
+- VERDICT: changes requested; route `to-dev`. Rework must bind the actual interpreter runtime/load components through every identity phase, resolve Windows base-runtime semantics securely, and add native runtime-mutation and Windows wheel-in-venv coverage. Evidence: `TASK-260720-2g21eg_review-verdict-cycle-4.md` and its task-scoped JUnit outcomes.

